# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# **Every line names the same image**, which is not tidiness. These are five
# independent implementations asked the same question about the same file,
# and the whole value is in where they disagree. In one image per reference,
# with one base state each, a disagreement would first have to be shown not
# to be the images' fault. One image makes "the reference set" one fact.
#
# The image is built here, so the tag is not a digest of the finished image:
# two builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is
# pinned instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, the
# packages by full apt version, resolved against a dated snapshot of the
# Debian archive so the pins do not expire - and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. oracle_env.check_pin() therefore runs in *both*
# modes; there is no stock image named here, so relaxing it in host mode
# would leave nothing checking anything.
#
# Three of them need a word about what the number is:
#
#   - For libsndfile it is the **C library's** version and not
#     python-soundfile's. soundfile is a ctypes wrapper; libsndfile is what
#     decides an answer, and the two move independently.
#   - mutagen was added in phase 3, and the reason is worth keeping: ffmpeg
#     is what *generates* the tag fixtures, so scoring them with ffmpeg
#     alone would be asking one implementation whether it agrees with
#     itself. A misunderstanding held in both its writer and its reader is
#     invisible to that. mutagen shares no code with it.
#   - For flac it is the reference implementation's own version, and it is
#     the one pin here that covers three of the lines below. sox reaches
#     FLAC through libsox_fmt_flac, libsndfile links libFLAC, and `flac`
#     is libFLAC's front end - so **for FLAC there are two implementations
#     in this image, not five**: ffmpeg's native decoder, and libFLAC
#     answering three times. libflac14 is pinned in the Containerfile for
#     that reason. Do not read "three references agree about a FLAC file"
#     as corroboration; check_corpus.py groups them.
#   - For pywave it is the interpreter version, because `wave` is a stdlib
#     module with no version of its own and its behaviour has changed
#     between releases. Note what that same release took away: **Python 3.13
#     removed `aifc`** under PEP 594, so there is no stdlib AIFF reader any
#     more. AIFF is scored by ffmpeg, sox and libsndfile; WAV by all four.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and a
# new answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression
# to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   make oracle-build

ffmpeg	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	ffmpeg 7.1.5	libavformat's RIFF and IFF demuxers; the most widely deployed reader of both formats
sox	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	SoX 14.4.2	A separate lineage from ffmpeg, and the reference happiest being asked for a raw sample dump
libsndfile	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	libsndfile 1.2.2	The third parser, and the one most non-ffmpeg audio applications actually use
pywave	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	Python 3.13.5 wave	A pure-Python fourth reading that shares no code with the three C parsers. WAV only; 3.13 removed aifc
mutagen	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	mutagen 1.47.0	The only reference here that exists for metadata, and the implementation most taggers are built on. Tags only; it reads no samples
flac	localhost/ghoti-audio-oracle-refs:deb13	flac 1.5.0	The format's own reference implementation. `flac -t` verifies a file's STREAMINFO MD5, which scores the whole decoder at once; libFLAC underneath is also what sox and libsndfile answer FLAC questions with

